{"id": "14112", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=14112", "title": "Harlingen (Texas)", "text": "Harlingen is a city in Texas.\nUnderstand.\nHarlingen was envisioned by a man named Lon Hill in 1904 as a shipping and industrial center along the Rio Grande and named after the city of the same name in the Netherlands. Since then, it has really bloomed and grown population-wise, and has a few tourist attractions, but not a lot.\n* American Eagle/American Airlines to Dallas-Fort Worth, Chicago O'Hare\n* Southwest to Austin, Houston Hobby, Dallas love Field\n* Sun Country Seasonally to/from Cancun and Minneapolis\n* United Airlines/United Express to Houston, \n* Delta Seasonally to/from Minneapolis-St Paul\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nFor travelers not renting a car click here for a list of taxi and shuttle services to South Padre Island and locally in town. The Valley Metro #41 provides local public transportation into town from the airport.\nThe next nearest commercial airport for Aeromexico, Volaris, Delta (to Austin), and Allegiant is in McAllen, 45 min west on I-2/US 83. Both United &amp; American/American Eagle serve both Valley International Airport and McAllen from their respective hubs in Houston and Dallas-Ft Worth."}
{"id": "14118", "revid": "35579", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=14118", "title": "Haro", "text": "Haro is a city of 11,000 people (2018) in La Rioja. It produces red wine, and hosts the annual Haro Wine Festival. Its architectural heritage includes the plateresque main entrance of the Church of Santo Tom\u00e1s, the work of Felipe Vigarny, numerous palaces, and the old town, which has been declared a Historic-Artistic Site.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nPau Pyr\u00e9n\u00e9es Airport across the border in France is roughly a 3-hour drive from Haro.\nBy car.\nIf you are heading over the Pyrenees from France then Haro makes a good first stop.\nBy boat.\nThe port of Santander is approximately 2 hours drive away.\nGet around.\nHaro is a small town and you should be able to cover it entirely on foot. For many of the bodegas you'll want a bike, car, or taxi. \nDo.\nVisit one of the many \"bodegas\" just outside of town. Many of them have guided tours in English but most require prior booking, even if it's just calling half an hour before the tour. The tourist office can provide a list of the bodegas, their contact details and prices. Many are near the train station.\nFestivals.\nThe best time to visit Haro is for the major event of the year: The Batalla Del Vino. Taking place every St Pedro\u2019s Day, 29 June, the week\u2019s festivities reach their climax when hundreds of people, dressed from top-to-toe in white with red scarves, hike up to a nearby hill, have a quick church service and then chuck red wine at each other until they are white no more. Walking through the streets is like a ribena berry overdose. Apparently it\u2019s all to do with land and a dispute with a neighbouring town that dates back to the 10th century.\nEat.\nHead through the narrow alleyways and you will find a number of charming courtyards home to a variety of intimate tapas bars. \nDrink.\nWine is the name of the game in this town and you will find many places to indulge in the bar-lined alleyways that wind their way through the town from the main square."}
{"id": "14119", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=14119", "title": "Harpenden", "text": "Harpenden is a town in Hertfordshire near the border of Bedfordshire. It is between and near to the city of St Albans and Luton. Primarily a residential community, in the outside of the London commuter belt, but if passing through, there are interesting sights and pleasant places to be enjoyed in this town.\nHarpenden is built around, the prime area of Harpenden, with the High Street and Station to its north and Southdown to its south.\nGet in.\nBy train.\n is served by Thameslink from London St Pancras and Luton Airport Parkway. Thameslink also serves Gatwick Airport, with direct journeys only operating at certain times. At times of limited direct services, it is possible to change at London St Pancras onto another Thameslink service northbound towards Bedford.\nBy car.\nHarpenden is equidistant between St Albans and Luton by J10 of the M1 5 mi via the A1081 [formally called the A6] the London to Bedford road. The nearest motorway is M1 J9 near Redbourn. \nBy bus.\nRoute 307 from Redbourn. \nRoute 321 from Watford to Luton via St Albans.\nRoute 610 from Luton to Potters Bar calling at: Wheathampstead, Hatfield and Brookmans Park.\nGet around.\nLocal bus companies operate services around the town and connect Harpenden to Luton, St Albans, Welwyn Garden City, Hatfield and other nearby towns, but the most advisable way to navigate Harpenden is on foot.\nIf you have a lot of luggage or a number of people travelling in your party then booking Harpenden Taxis will make the price more affordable.\nBuy.\nDespite the two main up-market supermarkets: \nHarpenden has some interesting shops:\nEat.\nPrices of all these restaurants above are reasonable, but remember that dining out in Britain is not a particularly cheap option.\nDrink.\nCentre.\nIn Harpenden High Street:\nOn the west side of Harpenden Common:"}
{"id": "14120", "revid": "38175", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=14120", "title": "Harper", "text": "Harper is a city in Southern Liberia.\nUnderstand.\nAt the south east of the country, Harper is the former capital of Maryland. It is known for its beautiful beaches and beach houses. Now these houses are dilapidated, but it's still possible to get a sense of the glory of the past.\nGet around.\nYou can wave down virtually any motorbike without a passenger to get a ride. For L$20, you can go to any part of the main city.\nThe same motorbikes will take you to Pleebo for L$250. Alternatively, you can take a commercial car. There are trucks that charge about L$150 per person.\nEat.\nThere are little coffee shacks setup around Harper. They sell eggs (cooked with local spices) with bread, instant coffee, spam, sardines, and spaghetti in a local spicy sauce. Great if you are missing western-type foods."}
{"id": "14123", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=14123", "title": "Harpers Ferry", "text": "Harpers Ferry is a historic town of 285 people (2020) in the northern corner of the Shenandoah Valley just inside West Virginia. It is a natural transportation hub: the Shenandoah River empties into the Potomac there. Three states meet there. East from Harpers Ferry, over the Shenandoah, is Virginia. North, across the Potomac, is Maryland.\nUnderstand.\nHarpers Ferry is, for its size, as full of history as any town in the United States, with the possible exception of Gettysburg, Pennsylvania. A lot happened in Harper's Ferry (as it was spelled until the 20th century). Nearly half a million people visit the Harpers Ferry National Historical Park each year.\nIn 1859 Harper's Ferry was the location of the largest episode of guerrilla warfare in American history up to that point. A group of abolitionists led by John Brown attacked an armory in the town, hoping to get weapons for a slave revolt. There was a battle with Brown and his followers on the one side, and federal troops commanded by Robert E. Lee on the other side. While the attack failed and Brown was hanged shortly thereafter, it is said to have contributed greatly to the outbreak of the American Civil War in 1861.\nVisitors to Harpers Ferry should be aware that the topic of John Brown is a sensitive one. He was not seen as a hero to the pro-slavery white population.\nA comic historical novel about Brown's raid on Harper's Ferry is Flashman and the Angel of the Lord.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nHarpers Ferry sits at the junction of the Potomac and Shenandoah Rivers, where the states of West Virginia, Maryland, and Virginia meet. To reach it, take Interstate 70 to Frederick Maryland, and exit on US 340 west for about 15 miles.\nWalk.\nHarpers Ferry is on the Appalachian Trail and the C &amp; O Canal trail. Since it is at the crossroads of these two trails, it is an extremely popular starting or ending point for backpackers."}
{"id": "14127", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=14127", "title": "Harrietville", "text": "Harrietville is a village in the High Country of Australia.\nGet in.\nHarrietville is accessible by road on the Great Alpine Road from Bright (20 kilometers) or Mount Hotham (20 kilometers).\nThe Alps Link is a government bus service from Bright to Omeo via Harrietville, Mt Hotham, Dinner Plain and Cobungra on Wednesday, Friday and Sunday.\nThe Snowball Express[dead link] bus service runs to Harrietville from Mount Hotham, Bright, Myrtleford and Melbourne during the snow season.\nBuy.\nHarrietville no longer has fuel available, so remember to buy it in Bright before the ascent to Mount Hotham and Dinner Plain, since fuel is not available on the mountain or Harrietville:\nIf travelling into the mountains past Harrietville towards Mount Hotham, you will be required to carry fitting snow chains in winter. These are available for hire in Harrietville:"}
